================================================================================
Bundled model weights
================================================================================

The trained model weights distributed alongside this project (as release
assets, not in the source tree) are AGPL-3.0, as part of this combined work.

  - The timer and character detectors are YOLOv8 models fine-tuned from
    Ultralytics' COCO-pretrained checkpoints, which are AGPL-3.0; the derived
    weights are therefore AGPL-3.0 too.
  - The m_char classifier is a ResNet-18 fine-tuned from torchvision's ImageNet
    weights (BSD-3-Clause), redistributed under AGPL-3.0 as part of this
    combined work. torchvision's copyright notice is retained in
    licenses/torchvision-LICENSE.txt.


================================================================================
Third-party components
================================================================================

This project depends on a lot of open-source software at runtime, each piece
used under its own license. The full license text of every bundled dependency
ships with the application in one generated document:

    licenses/THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.txt

It is written from the installed wheels at build time by
tools/licensing/license_report.py, and is reachable in the app from
Help ▸ About ▸ Licenses. From a checkout:

    python tools/licensing/license_report.py bundle   # write the shipped document
    python tools/licensing/license_report.py report   # print name -> license, to audit

The dependencies whose licenses constrain how this project may be distributed:

  Ultralytics (YOLOv8) .............. AGPL-3.0
  ultralytics-thop .................. AGPL-3.0
  PyQt6 ............................. GPL-3.0-only
  Qt 6 (PyQt6-Qt6) .................. LGPL-3.0
  python-bidi ....................... LGPL-3.0
  OpenCV's FFmpeg video backend ..... LGPL-2.1 (see the FFmpeg section below)
  The bundled FFmpeg binaries ....... GPL (see the FFmpeg section below)
  certifi, tqdm ..................... MPL-2.0

The two AGPL-3.0 dependencies are why this project is itself AGPL-3.0. PyQt6
(GPL-3.0-only) and Qt 6 (LGPL-3.0) are both compatible with that (GPL-3.0
section 13 permits combining with an AGPL-3.0 work, and AGPL-3.0 section 13
the reverse). The LGPL components (Qt 6, python-bidi, OpenCV's FFmpeg backend)
ship as dynamically linked libraries, which satisfies the LGPL's relinking
requirement. certifi and tqdm are MPL-2.0, weak copyleft: distributing a binary
means saying where their source is (section 3.2), and the pointers under
"Corresponding source" below do that. Everything else in the dependency closure
is permissive (MIT, BSD, Apache-2.0, PSF and similar); their copyright notices
travel in the bundled document.

One more component ships in the packaged application without being a Python
dependency: PyInstaller's bootloader, the small C program that becomes the
executable's entry point. It is GPL-2.0-or-later with the bootloader exception,
which expressly permits linking it into an application under any license;
source at https://github.com/pyinstaller/pyinstaller. As a build tool rather
than an installed package it is absent from the generated document.


================================================================================
Bundled FFmpeg binaries
================================================================================

The packaged application bundles FFmpeg so end users need nothing installed.
The ffmpeg executable is invoked as a separate process (via ffmpeg-python
building a command line), never linked into this program; the shared libraries
are used by OpenCV and Qt for decoding. Several distinct FFmpeg builds ship,
and they differ by platform. Every one arrives from a published wheel or
release rather than from the build machine, and none carries --enable-nonfree
(the build refuses to proceed past one that does), so every copy is freely
redistributable.

The configure flags actually read out of these binaries at build time are
recorded in licenses/FFMPEG-BUILD-CONFIG.txt, which ships beside this file.
Where that document and this one disagree, that document describes the artifact
you have; the notes below describe the wheels this project builds against. Of
those, only opencv-python is version-pinned by build_mac.sh /
build_windows.ps1 — it is the one whose vendored FFmpeg has differed in LICENSE
and not merely in version between releases (see 3).

  1. bin/ffmpeg(.exe) - the executable this program actually calls, staged from
     the imageio-ffmpeg package by the build scripts. Only this copy ships: the
     package itself, which carries the same binary, is left out of the bundle.
       Windows (v7.1) / Linux (v7.0.2): --enable-gpl --enable-version3
       -> GPL-3.0.
       macOS (v7.1): --enable-gpl without --enable-version3 -> GPL-2.0-or-later
       (that build omits the Apache-licensed AMR codecs which force version3 on
       the other two). FFmpeg is licensed "version 2 or later", so this copy is
       likewise usable under GPL-3.0.

  2. Qt's multimedia backend (avcodec, avformat, avutil, swscale, swresample),
     used by the in-app video preview. Qt builds its own FFmpeg (n7.1.3) on
     both platforms, without --enable-gpl, so these copies are LGPL. They are
     distinct from the OpenCV libraries in (3) despite the overlapping soname.

  3. OpenCV's video I/O backend.
       Windows and Linux: OpenCV's own builds, no --enable-gpl -> LGPL-2.1,
       whose text opencv-python ships in its LICENSE-3RD-PARTY.txt.
       macOS: opencv-python's macOS wheels instead vendor a Homebrew-built
       FFmpeg 7.1.1 configured --enable-gpl --enable-version3 -> GPL-3.0. That
       is a property of the published wheel, not of the build machine: the same
       libraries, byte for byte, are inside
       opencv_python-4.13.0.92-cp37-abi3-macosx_13_0_arm64.whl as downloaded
       from PyPI. This is why the build scripts pin opencv-python: an upstream
       wheel carrying a differently configured FFmpeg would otherwise silently
       change the licensing of what ships.

In a source checkout no FFmpeg is bundled: the binary is located at runtime as
an `ffmpeg` already on PATH (e.g. a system or Homebrew install), otherwise the
static build provided by the imageio-ffmpeg dependency.


